1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,590
Talk to the Book turns a spoken memory
into a first draft of your memoir.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:07,100
Here's the whole loop, start to finish.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,064
I upload a voice memo, rambling, false
starts and all.

4
00:00:15,064 --> 00:00:18,450
ElevenLabs Scribe turns it into a
transcript.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,790
Then Gemma, an open model, cleans it up
and files it.

6
00:00:28,670 --> 00:00:33,117
Gemma strips the filler, keeps every
detail, and invents nothing.

7
00:00:33,117 --> 00:00:36,606
It files the memory into the right part of
my book,

8
00:00:36,606 --> 00:00:38,590
Part One, Breaking into Tech,

9
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,220
with a suggested section and a reason.

10
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:44,670
The draft is editable before anything else
happens.

11
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:52,250
Once I approve it, I pick a voice, and
ElevenLabs reads my draft back to me.

12
00:00:54,110 --> 00:00:56,300
I found a lot of opportunities through
Twitter.

13
00:00:56,670 --> 00:00:58,202
I had built a community there,

14
00:00:58,202 --> 00:01:01,010
and I was tuned in to the tech
conversations happening.

15
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:05,920
One opportunity that really caught my eye
was a post by Jessica Livingston.

16
00:01:06,490 --> 00:01:08,775
She was the co-founder of Y Combinator,

17
00:01:08,775 --> 00:01:12,700
and she wrote a blog post about wanting to
see more women founders.

18
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:17,170
She was going to sponsor a program that
taught women how to code over the summer

19
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:20,600
in partnership with Lambda School, which
was…

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,830
Hearing your own words read aloud is the
fastest edit there is.

21
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:30,199
And because narration only happens after
approval,

22
00:01:30,199 --> 00:01:33,700
no credits are spent on a draft you
haven't signed off on.

23
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,260
Saved drafts stay on my device, filed
under the book's four-part outline.

24
00:01:40,890 --> 00:01:43,764
Run it locally, and Gemma drafts on your
own machine,

25
00:01:43,764 --> 00:01:46,800
so an unpublished memoir never has to
leave your laptop.

26
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,100
Talk to the Book. Because talking is
lazier than typing.
